* AIF-149. THE REFUSAL NAMES WHERE THE AREA IS. Explicit-run, mints_catalog.
* Self-bootstrapping in SANDBOX, self-erasing. Owner ruling 2026-08-30.
*
* WHAT THIS PROTECTS. add_relation() resolves both endpoints through a resolver
* SCOPED to the current workspace, so an area open in ANOTHER workspace is not
* refused -- it is INVISIBLE, and until 22991263e the refusal reported
* invisibility as absence: "add failed (parent/child not open)" for an area
* that IS open. Nothing decided that a relation may not cross a workspace. The
* FEATURE is parked with the gate open; this spec guards the HARDENING half,
* which is true whichever way the feature eventually goes.
*
* AND THE SILENCE UNDER THE SILENCE, which is what made this worth a spec.
* Every add-failure path reported through emit_rel_diag, which opens
* `if (!g_verbose) return;`, and g_verbose defaults to
* DOTTALK_EXTRA_DIAGNOSTICS -- ON under DOTTALK_PROFILE=DEV, OFF under PROD
* (CMakeLists.txt:172-177) -- while the caller is a bare
* `if (!add_relation(...)) return;` (cmd_relations.cpp:500). So on a PROD build
* a failing SET RELATION could print NOTHING AT ALL. Refusals now go through
* emit_rel_refusal, which is NOT verbosity-gated.
*
* THIS SPEC RUNS UNDER DEV AND THEREFORE CANNOT TELL THE TWO APART. Under DEV
* both the old and the new code speak, so T1 and T2 discriminate the WORDING
* and not the GATING. Naming this rather than implying it: proving the PROD
* half needs a PROD-profile build, which no run has yet produced. The wording
* arms are still the ones that catch a regression, because collapsing the two
* messages back together is the likely edit.
*
* HOW IT ASSERTS, AND WHY NOT BY FIELD VALUE. relation_workspace_scope.dts
* asserts by field value "never by console text", and that is the right default.
* It cannot be followed here: the thing under test IS A REFUSAL, and a refusal
* writes no field. So T1/T2/T3 are TRANSCRIPT-READ arms in the RELSCAN idiom --
* a marked block that MUST CONTAIN a line, and for T2 a block that MUST NOT.
* G0 and G1 are field-value guards, and they carry the weight the arms cannot:
* if G1 reds, the relation machinery is broken and the refusal arms prove
* nothing about scoping.
*
* WHAT THIS IS NOT, because a neighbour exists and the names look alike.
* relation_parent_workspace_crossing.dts (AIF-137, spec RELWSNAME) asserts that
* the relation resolvers do NOT reach across workspaces -- they were
* name-addressed and unscoped, so a refresh issued in one workspace drove
* another workspace's child, and scoping them is the fix that spec guards.
* THIS SPEC DOES NOT RE-OPEN THAT. It asserts only that the REFUSAL says where
* the area is. The unscoped lookup behind the message REPORTS and never
* RESOLVES: add_relation reads a handle from it for the sentence and returns
* false either way. If a future edit uses that lookup as the endpoint, AIF-137
* comes back and RELWSNAME is the spec that should red, not this one.
*
* READ RULE -- five blocks, and the NEGATIVE in T2 is half the value:
*   XWSREL-T1  MUST contain "is open in workspace XWSB"
*   XWSREL-T2  MUST contain "not open"
*              MUST NOT contain "is open in workspace"
*   XWSREL-T3  see its own note; UNVERIFIED until the first run
* Collapse the two messages into one and T2 gains a sentence it must not have.
* Revert the scoped-resolver reporting and T1 loses one it must.
SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE DESTROY XWSB
WORKSPACE DESTROY XWSA
ERASE xwsap CONFIRM
ERASE xwsac CONFIRM
ERASE xwsbc CONFIRM
FORMULA "XWSREL-REGRESSION-BEGIN"
REL CLEAR ALL
SELECT 0
CREATE X64 XWSAP (PK C(4))
APPEND
REPLACE PK WITH "A"
APPEND
REPLACE PK WITH "B"
SELECT 1
CREATE X64 XWSAC (CK C(4), CLBL C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE CK WITH "A"
REPLACE CLBL WITH "A_ALPHA"
APPEND
REPLACE CK WITH "B"
REPLACE CLBL WITH "A_BETA"
SELECT 2
CREATE X64 XWSBC (CK C(4), CLBL C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE CK WITH "A"
REPLACE CLBL WITH "B_ALPHA"
SELECT 1
GO 2
? "XWSREL_G0_child_fixture_written:" + (ALLTRIM(CLBL) = "A_BETA")
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* TWO WORKSPACES. A holds the parent and its own child; B holds the table the
* cross attempt will name. Areas opened after a SWITCH join that workspace.
WORKSPACE NEW XWSA
WORKSPACE SWITCH XWSA
USE xwsap IN FREE
USE xwsac IN FREE
WORKSPACE NEW XWSB
WORKSPACE SWITCH XWSB
USE xwsbc IN FREE
WORKSPACE SWITCH XWSA
* G1 -- THE RELATION MACHINERY IS LIVE, BY FIELD VALUE. Also the counterweight
* for the change that ungated refusals: a same-workspace ADD must still SUCCEED.
* If this reds, every arm below is UNPROVEN -- a refusal that prints for the
* wrong reason reads exactly like one that prints for the right reason.
SELECT xwsap
REL ADD XWSAP XWSAC ON PK TO CK
GO 1
REL REFRESH
SELECT xwsac
? "XWSREL_G1_same_workspace_relation_slaves:" + (ALLTRIM(CLBL) = "A_ALPHA")
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* T1 -- THE DISCRIMINATOR. XWSBC is OPEN, in XWSB, and invisible from here.
* The refusal must say so and must name the workspace.
SELECT xwsap
FORMULA "XWSREL-T1-BEGIN"
REL ADD XWSAP XWSBC ON PK TO CK
FORMULA "XWSREL-T1-END"
* T2 -- THE NEGATIVE, AND IT IS WHY T1 MEANS ANYTHING. XWSGONE is open NOWHERE.
* A build that answers "open in workspace" here has not distinguished the two
* conditions, it has merely renamed one message.
FORMULA "XWSREL-T2-BEGIN"
REL ADD XWSAP XWSGONE ON PK TO CK
FORMULA "XWSREL-T2-END"
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* T3 -- THE EDGE-SKIP LATCH, AND IT IS UNVERIFIED UNTIL THIS SPEC FIRST RUNS.
* refresh_from_parent_name used a bare `if (!child) continue;`, so a refresh
* that propagated down NONE of its edges printed what a complete one printed.
* It now trips the relation truncation latch, naming the edge.
*
* THE FIXTURE MAY OR MAY NOT REACH IT, and that is stated rather than assumed:
* the edge survives only if closing an AREA leaves the relation in the store.
* If it does not, this block is EMPTY and that is a fact about CLOSE, not a
* failure of the fix -- read it that way and say so, do not credit or condemn
* the latch on it. G2 immediately below proves the edge was live one statement
* before the close, so an empty T3 cannot be blamed on a dead fixture.
SELECT xwsap
GO 2
REL REFRESH
SELECT xwsac
? "XWSREL_G2_edge_live_before_the_close:" + (ALLTRIM(CLBL) = "A_BETA")
SELECT xwsac
CLOSE
SELECT xwsap
FORMULA "XWSREL-T3-BEGIN"
GO 1
REL REFRESH
FORMULA "XWSREL-T3-END"
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* TEARDOWN. Leaves no workspace declared and no table behind. Restoring the
* relation store matters as much as the tables: a spec that leaves residue
* poisons everything downstream of it.
REL CLEAR ALL
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE DESTROY XWSB
WORKSPACE DESTROY XWSA
ERASE xwsap CONFIRM
ERASE xwsac CONFIRM
ERASE xwsbc CONFIRM
FORMULA "XWSREL-REGRESSION-END"
